= The Long Life of Paper

Paper is so ordinary that we rarely stop to consider it, and yet it is one of
the *most consequential inventions* in the whole history of communication. It is
cheap, light, foldable, and extraordinarily durable when it is made well; a
sheet manufactured a thousand years ago can still be read today, while the
magnetic and optical media of the last century have already grown unreadable.
The story of how a mat of macerated plant fibre came to carry almost everything
humanity chose to remember is a story of /patient craftsmanship/, of secrets kept
and secrets stolen, and of a technology so successful that it became invisible.

A note on the marks themselves: the asterisk in 5 * 3 and the slash in a date
like 10/105, or in a phrase like paper and/or parchment, are ordinary text and
must never open emphasis. Only a delimiter that hugs a word does, so *this* is
strong and /this/ is emphasised while the arithmetic and the dates are left alone.

This short account follows the sheet from the materials that preceded it,
through its invention and the closely guarded craft of its manufacture, to the
question that preoccupies conservators today: how to keep the paper of the
industrial age from destroying itself.

== Before the Sheet

Long before paper, people wrote on whatever would hold a mark. The Mesopotamians
pressed wedge-shaped characters into damp clay and baked the tablets hard, which
is counterintuitively why so much of their administration survives: a city that
burned preserved its archives by firing them. The Egyptians beat the pith of the
papyrus reed into flat sheets, laying strips at right angles and pounding them
until the plant's own sap glued them into a single surface. Papyrus gave us the
word for paper, but it is not paper; it is a laminated plant, not a suspension of
loose fibres reconstituted into a sheet.

Parchment, made from the scraped and stretched skin of sheep, goats and calves,
was the great writing surface of the European Middle Ages. It was strong,
reusable, and beautiful, and it was also ruinously expensive, for a single large
book might consume the skins of an entire flock. A material this dear kept
literacy the preserve of institutions rich enough to afford it. What the world
was waiting for, without quite knowing it, was a writing surface that could be
manufactured in quantity from cheap and renewable materials, and manufactured by
anyone who learned the craft.

== The Invention in the East

That surface was invented in China. Tradition credits the court official Cai Lun
with the innovation around the year 105, though archaeology now shows that crude
paper existed for perhaps two centuries before him; his contribution was most
likely a refinement and a championing at court rather than a creation from
nothing. The essential insight was startlingly simple and yet had eluded every
earlier civilisation: if you reduce plant material to its individual fibres,
suspend those fibres in water, and then lift them out on a fine screen, the
fibres settle into an interlocking mat that dries into a strong, flexible,
writable sheet.

The early Chinese papermakers used the inner bark of the mulberry, along with
hemp rags, old fishing nets, and other fibrous waste. These were pounded and
retted until the cellulose broke down into a pulp, and the quality of the pulp
determined the quality of the paper. The craft spread slowly and secretively
across Asia over the following centuries, reaching Korea and Japan, where it was
refined to an extraordinary delicacy, and travelling west along the trade routes
towards the frontiers of the Islamic world.

== The Craft of the Mould

At the heart of traditional papermaking stands a deceptively simple tool: the
mould and deckle. The mould is a rectangular frame across which a screen is
stretched, historically of thin bamboo strips or fine wire; the deckle is a
removable rim that sits on top of the mould and holds the pulp within the sheet's
intended edges. The papermaker dips the mould into a vat of dilute pulp, lifts it
out level and dripping, and gives it a practised shake that felts the fibres
together and distributes them evenly across the screen.

The shake is the whole art, and it cannot really be taught in words. Too little,
and the fibres lie all in one direction, giving a weak and characterless sheet;
too much, and the pulp is thrown unevenly, leaving the sheet thick in one corner
and translucent in another. A skilled worker could form a sheet every few
seconds, hour after hour, each one indistinguishable from the last. The newly
formed sheet was then couched, or pressed off the mould onto a felt, stacked with
its fellows, squeezed under a great screw press to drive out the water, and hung
to dry. Finally it might be sized with gelatine so that ink would not feather,
and burnished smooth by calendering between hard surfaces.

The parallel lines visible when an old sheet is held to the light, the so-called
chain-lines and laid-lines, are the shadow of the mould's own screen, printed
into the very structure of the paper by the settling of the fibres. They are an
accidental signature of the manufacturing process, and to the trained eye they
reveal a great deal about where and when a sheet was made.

== Watermarks and the Honest Sheet

Papermakers soon discovered that a design twisted in wire and sewn onto the mould
screen would leave a thinner, more translucent line in the finished sheet, a mark
visible only when the paper was held against the light. This was the watermark,
and it began as a straightforward maker's signature, a way for a particular mill
to identify its own production and defend its reputation for quality.

The watermark quickly acquired other uses. It certified the size and grade of a
sheet in an age before standardised measurements; it defeated counterfeiting,
since a convincing forgery of a watermarked banknote required not merely printing
but the reconstruction of the entire papermaking apparatus; and it became, in
time, a small and characteristically understated art form in its own right.
Watermarking is one of those rare security technologies that is both beautiful
and genuinely difficult to reproduce, and it survives in our banknotes and our
finest stationery to this day, a direct and unbroken inheritance from the medieval
mill.

== Permanence and Decay

There is a bitter irony at the end of this story. The handmade papers of the
medieval and early modern periods, formed from cotton and linen rags and sized
with gelatine, are chemically stable and can survive intact for many centuries.
The machine-made papers of the industrial era, produced from wood pulp by
processes that leave acidic residues in the fibre, are not. They yellow, grow
brittle, and eventually crumble, destroying themselves through slow internal
combustion of a chemical rather than a fiery kind.

This is the problem of what conservators call the brittle-book crisis. Vast
quantities of nineteenth and twentieth-century printing, the very record of the
modern age, are quietly disintegrating on library shelves. The remedies are
laborious and expensive: mass deacidification to neutralise the acids, careful
environmental control of temperature and humidity, and, where the original can no
longer be saved, reformatting onto some more durable medium. It is a curious
reflection that the cheap and abundant paper which democratised the written word
should prove so much less permanent than the dear and scarce materials it
replaced.

The lesson is not that the old ways were always better, for the medieval book was
a luxury few could ever hold. It is rather that permanence is a property to be
designed for, deliberately and at some cost, and never simply assumed. Paper
taught us to remember on an unprecedented scale; the task that remains is to make
sure that what we have written does not, through mere neglect of its material,
forget itself.
